      <!-- Section 1: The Geography of Istanbul -->
      <h2>1. Decoding Istanbul's Geography: Europe vs Asia &amp; The Waterways</h2>

      <p>To navigate Istanbul like a native, picture the city divided into three distinct landmasses bounded by two bodies of water:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>1. The Historic Peninsula (South of Golden Horn):</strong> Sultanahmet, Eminönü, and Fatih. This is ancient Byzantium/Constantinople, surrounded by Roman sea walls and crowned by the imperial domes of the Sultans.</li>
        <li><strong>2. The Modern European Side (North of Golden Horn):</strong> Karaköy, Galata, Beyoğlu, and Beşiktaş. Connected to the old city by the iconic Galata Bridge. This area is the modern cultural, financial, and culinary hub.</li>
        <li><strong>3. The Asian Side (Anatolia, Across the Bosphorus):</strong> Kadıköy and Üsküdar. Reached in 20 glorious minutes by public passenger ferry. It is authentic, residential, vibrant, and largely unmarred by mass tourist groups.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="roam-callout-ai">
        <strong>💡 The Local Secret of Istanbul Transit:</strong> Never take taxis across the Bosphorus bridges during rush hour. <strong>The iconic yellow-and-white public passenger ferries (Şehir Hatları)</strong> cost less than €0.80, bypass 100% of road traffic, and offer the most cinematic, breeze-swept commute on the planet.
      </div>

