      <!-- Section 8: FAQ Accordion -->
      <h2>8. Frequently Asked Questions About Staying in Istanbul</h2>

      <div class="faq-accordion">
        <div class="faq-item active">
          <h3 class="faq-question">Is Istanbul safe for international tourists in 2026?</h3>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>Yes, Istanbul is overwhelmingly safe for tourists. Violent crime is rare across central neighborhoods. The primary safety concerns are petty scams: shoe-shine brush drop tricks, fake friendly locals inviting solo men to overpriced nightclub drinks (the classic bar scam), and taxi drivers refusing meters. Standard street awareness ensures a trouble-free holiday.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3 class="faq-question">How much does a hotel in central Istanbul cost?</h3>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>Istanbul offers phenomenal luxury value. Clean, well-located 3-star boutique hotels range from <strong>€60 to €110 per night</strong>; characterful 4-star boutique townhouse stays in Karaköy or Galata range from <strong>€120 to €220 per night</strong>; while 5-star Bosphorus waterfront palace hotels range from <strong>€450 to €900+ per night</strong>.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3 class="faq-question">What is the Istanbulkart and how do I get one?</h3>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>The <strong>Istanbulkart</strong> is the universal contactless transit card valid on all public ferries, trams, metros, funiculars, and buses across both continents. You can purchase and top up an anonymous plastic card at yellow vending machines at all major metro stations and ferry docks (cash TL required).</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3 class="faq-question">What is the dress code for visiting mosques in Istanbul?</h3>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>All visitors must remove shoes before entering carpeted prayer halls. Men must wear pants that cover the knees and shirts covering the shoulders. Women must wear clothing covering shoulders, knees, and a headscarf (hijab) covering hair. Free loaner headscarves and robes are available at major mosque entrances (Hagia Sophia, Blue Mosque).</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3 class="faq-question">Do I need an e-Visa for Turkey in 2026?</h3>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>Citizens of the United States, Canada, United Kingdom, and European Union countries can enter Turkey visa-free for tourist stays up to 90 days within any 180-day period. Always check official consular advisories before departing.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

